1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,972
A PostgreSQL query optimiser that proves what would fix your slow plan.

2
00:00:06,267 --> 00:00:12,146
Every visualiser tells you what happened. None tell you what to do — or check that it worked.

3
00:00:12,450 --> 00:00:16,011
A query against 4,000,000 orders.

4
00:00:16,181 --> 00:00:21,057
Executed in a read-only transaction — measured, not guessed.

5
00:00:21,367 --> 00:00:28,630
It read 4.00M rows to return 621.

6
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:35,197
99.7% of the work, thrown away by a filter.

7
00:00:35,483 --> 00:00:41,241
The plan as a graph — rows flow upward, leaves at the bottom.

8
00:00:41,411 --> 00:00:47,292
Edge thickness is row volume. Waste you can see.

9
00:00:47,462 --> 00:00:52,719
The slowest operation is ringed HOTSPOT — not left to inference.

10
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:57,770
Click any node: what it does, what it cost, how it fails.

11
00:00:58,083 --> 00:01:07,761
A flame graph can’t rank nodes — every ancestor is full-width. That’s why the list exists.

12
00:01:08,067 --> 00:01:12,181
Anyone can show you a slow query. Here’s the part nobody does.

13
00:01:12,483 --> 00:01:18,182
It suggests an index — then re-plans against a hypothetical one. Nothing is built.

14
00:01:18,352 --> 00:01:24,435
Estimated cost 87,748 → 422. Down 99.5%.

15
00:01:24,733 --> 00:01:34,033
It says what it did not prove: an estimate, not a measured speedup.

16
00:01:34,350 --> 00:01:39,440
Wrong column → unchanged. Never improved.

17
00:01:39,610 --> 00:01:43,411
Every verdict kept — with its numbers.

18
00:01:43,733 --> 00:01:49,882
24 operations Postgres can pick. Which is right, and when?

19
00:01:50,183 --> 00:01:56,269
A reference for all 24 — starting where most slow queries are decided.

20
00:01:56,439 --> 00:02:03,451
Seq Scan: right for most of a table. Ruinous for 621 of 4M.

21
00:02:03,621 --> 00:02:10,959
Bitmap Heap Scan: the middle ground between the two.

22
00:02:11,129 --> 00:02:17,247
Each card: what it does · why the planner picks it · what goes wrong.

23
00:02:17,417 --> 00:02:26,794
Joins, aggregates, sorts, parallelism — from the same glossary the narrator reads.

24
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:32,718
One idea: plan against a world, change it, re-plan, diff.

25
00:02:33,017 --> 00:02:37,566
Anti-patterns from the real Postgres parser, not a regex.

26
00:02:37,736 --> 00:02:46,987
NOT IN → NOT EXISTS is the fix and can change your results.

27
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:53,525
For 5 patterns it doesn&#8217;t stop at advice — it writes the query.

28
00:02:53,817 --> 00:03:01,433
A hidden N+1 — a subquery per row. The advisor writes the join.

29
00:03:01,603 --> 00:03:09,501
A unique index means the join cannot fan out — cited by name from pg_index.

30
00:03:09,671 --> 00:03:18,994
Cost 165,892 → 10,128 · 19,999 rows compared — identical.

31
00:03:19,300 --> 00:03:25,918
Its own rewrite, tested: Made it worse — do not apply.

32
00:03:26,088 --> 00:03:31,794
47,912 rows matched — and it still says no. Evidence over enthusiasm.

33
00:03:32,117 --> 00:03:35,686
Ranked by total time, not mean.

34
00:03:35,856 --> 00:03:45,472
0.72ms x 12,000 calls beats the 708ms report.

35
00:03:45,767 --> 00:03:53,015
When did this get slower, and what changed?

36
00:03:53,350 --> 00:03:56,945
A migration drops an index a hot query depended on.

37
00:03:57,115 --> 00:04:01,759
The build fails before it ships.

38
00:04:02,067 --> 00:04:08,081
Find it. Prove it. Ship it.
